{
  "title": "Heart failure: contraction versus filling",
  "sources": [
    "CH32S89",
    "CH32S90",
    "CH32S92",
    "CH32S93",
    "CH32S94",
    "CH32S95"
  ],
  "stages": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "title": "Compare the mechanism",
      "text": "Heart failure is a supply problem with different mechanical routes. We use the same scholarly ventricular anatomy for both comparisons. The moving volume markers are schematic, not measured chamber volumes."
    },
    {
      "start": 12.3,
      "title": "Reduced ejection",
      "text": "In systolic failure, impaired contraction reduces ejection. More blood remains after the beat, and stroke volume falls. The chapter connects reduced output with activation of compensatory systems."
    },
    {
      "start": 24.52,
      "title": "Restricted filling",
      "text": "In diastolic failure, relaxation and compliance are abnormal. Filling is restricted even when ejection fraction is preserved. A preserved fraction does not guarantee that enough blood enters and leaves the ventricle."
    },
    {
      "start": 38.0,
      "title": "Locate congestion",
      "text": "The slides distinguish pulmonary congestion in left failure from systemic venous congestion in right failure. Orthopnea and pulmonary edema belong to the pulmonary side; jugular venous distension and peripheral edema identify systemic congestion. Chapter thirty two, slides eighty nine, ninety and ninety two through ninety five, support the comparison. Specific diagnostic cutoffs and treatment selection require additional textbook evidence."
    }
  ],
